Assessment: Situated in the North Pennine &amp; Northumberland sandstone/whin upland within an active monument landscape (5 scheduled monuments within 2 km and 25 within 5 km, closest being Carrawburgh Roman fort and Hadrian's Wall and vallum between the field boundary east of the fort and the field boundary west of Coventina's Well in wall mile 31 (NHLE #1015914, 585 m W) and Hadrian's Wall and vallum between the road to Simonburn and the field boundary east of Carrawburgh car park in wall miles 29, 30 and 31 (NHLE #1010961, 937 m ENE)), the 1 m DTM isolates a sub-circular mound (1.08× elongation, 107 m² footprint, ~12 m diameter) rising +0.45 m above its 16 m local trend surface with weathered scarp gradients of 0.063 (3.6°), morphologically consistent with a Bronze Age bowl/bell barrow or upland funerary cairn. Correlated with The Megalithic Portal (megalithic.co.uk #51968: 'Carrawburgh Roman Fort', Stone Fort or Dun, 421.1 m). Bare-earth/surface comparison shows minimal above-ground vegetation or structure (nDSM 0.00 m), confirming that the anomaly is a ground-surface relief feature rather than scrub canopy. Critical false-positive &amp; HER check — Extractive/industrial mining heritage (nearby NHLE 'Carrawburgh Roman fort and Hadrian's Wall and vallum between the field boundary east of the fort and the field boundary west of Coventina's Well in wall mile 31'): coal or ironstone bell-pits (doughnut spoil rings around a shaft hollow), lead-mining hillocks, tin-streaming waste mounds, and post-medieval marl/gravel pits frequently mimic ring-barrows and hut circles.
